			<div class="content" id="post-content-1847118">
									

<p>First off, feel free to calm down. I've gotten worse responses from people I like less. I won't bite yer head off for stating an opinion.</p>
<p>Not being reeled in by it and thinking that it doesn't <em>feel</em> like an SCP is fine, but I would argue that it's substantially more than a &quot;particularly strange case of Multiple Personality Disorder&quot;. For one, let's take the genetic testing of the parents. Those tests are accurate enough that one false positive would be very strange. Either we have a hundred false positives somehow, or these people really <em>did</em> give birth to the exact same person. Like the article says, it's an infinitesimal probability that this is pure chance.</p>
<p>Now, as for the narratives. It's more than a weird case of MPD &#8212; this is what you would get if you took a variety of real, living people and smooshed them together into one person. All of those people would have their own paper trails, their own family and acquaintances &#8212; none of which are under any obligation to be consistent with each other. Of course, this <em>could</em> be explained as 1041 assimilating all of the appropriate personalities and information somehow, except A) none of those people are exactly <em>around</em> any more, and B) they were all 1041 to begin with, down to the thumb.</p>
<p>E: Just saw your edit. All is well.</p>

